name w5-04-escorted
timeout 400
time 2018-01-15T10:00:00Z

# CAMPAIGN W5, SORTIE 4 OF 10 - BALTIC AIR POLICING / QRA: IDENTIFICATION AS THE TASK.
# A BOMBER WITH A FIGHTER ESCORT - THREE CONTACTS, TWO INTERCEPTORS.
# Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/w5-baltic-qra.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
# CONTROL: none - its own cast.
#
# THE ARENA, and every coordinate in it is [SET]. NATO Baltic Air Policing flies out of Siauliai
# (Zokniai) AB, Lithuania, ~55.89 N 23.39 E [T4]; the intercept happens over the Baltic west of the
# Lithuanian coast, on a track running east toward the airspace boundary. Flown under `--elev const`
# on a 0 m datum and with NO TERRAIN MASKING (gap C4).
#   the coastal surveillance radar  a P-18, `set emcon free`, 55.80 N 21.05 E. GAP C1 IS CLOSED AND
#                                   THIS CAMPAIGN RE-CHECKED IT: W5's own spec still says "coastal
#                                   radars do not emit ... what the QRA protects is scenery". It is not
#                                   scenery any more - it radiates, it is heard on the intruder's
#                                   receiver, it builds its own tracks and it can be killed
#   the port                        Klaipeda, ~55.70 N 21.13 E [T4], a `target_hard`
#   the second coastal site         Palanga, ~55.92 N 21.07 E [T4], a `target_soft`
# NOTHING ON THE GROUND IS A TARGET IN THIS CAMPAIGN. They are what the intercept protects, and they
# are declared so the protection has objects. `objective protect` is deliberately NOT used: it names a
# UNIT, and a policing sortie protects an AREA.
# Scale at this latitude: 1 deg of latitude = 111 132 m, 1 deg of longitude = 62 411 m [DERIVED].
# THERE ARE NO SHIPS (gap C14) and the Baltic is full of them: the one cast item this campaign asks
# for and cannot have.
#
# THE SUBJECT IS A TRANSPORT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE. W5's spec lists this campaign as
# blocked on gap C7 - "no transport, ELINT or bomber module ... the subject of a real intercept is not
# a fighter, and substituting one changes the geometry, the closure and the whole point". RULE 7 SAYS
# RE-CHECK A BLOCKED MISSION AGAINST THE TREE AND NOT AGAINST THE GAP'S STATUS LINE, and the re-check
# says the capability is here: `an26` is a catalogue row (doc/modules/air/catalogue.md), it is a MOVER,
# and a mover has no generated deck - so the `ALPHA` verdict that keeps ten catalogue rows out of a
# campaign is a statement about GENERATED DECKS and there is none in this file. What the row has to do
# here is fly a straight line at a published speed, carry a transponder state and present a span to an
# eye; all three are sourced [T4]. It is also the anchor's Il-20 ELINT aircraft, because an ELINT
# aircraft RECEIVES and FlightBox has no ESM emission to model.
# ITS SPAN IS 29.3 m AND THAT IS THE NUMBER THIS CAMPAIGN TURNS ON: the eye resolves on presented
# extent, so the transport is IDENTIFIED where a fighter is not even recognised.
#
# WHAT IS DECLARED AND WHAT IS NOT. `objective identify` measures the GEOMETRY - a planar range held
# for a cumulative dwell - and never a sensor event, because producing "this unit discriminated that
# unit" would mean building a contact->unit correlation, and that function's EXISTENCE is the identity
# leak the tree is shaped to prevent (doc/missions/verdict.md). The sensor half is read BESIDE the
# verdict, out of `radar IFF_REPLY` and `vis RECOGNISED`/`vis IDENTIFIED`. THE PRICE IS KNOWN AND
# ACCEPTED: a pilot that flies the box with its eyes shut still scores - and sortie 09 turns that
# sentence into a measurement instead of a caveat. The box (2 000 m) and the dwell are [SET] per
# mission; there is no default, and W5's Knowledge 2 marks the abeam geometry [SET] because no source
# in the set publishes one. Aspect is deliberately not in the objective.
#
# THE STRUCTURAL DISCLOSURE, repeated in all ten files because a reader who skips it reads every
# result backwards:
#   1. THE PASS IS FLOWN BY ROUTE, NOT BY THE PILOT. `FBPilot` has no intercept-and-shadow behaviour,
#      and `set task intercept` is a SHOOTING task whose own machine calls this exact geometry an
#      ABORT: FBPilot.cpp:1040, "inside InterceptAbortRangeNm = 5.0 nm and never shot - that is no
#      longer an intercept". MEASURED in attribution run A1 (w5-baltic-qra.md, State): the identical
#      pass with `set task intercept` goes `eng_state=abort` at t = 5.1 s at 4.52 nm, turns away, and
#      identifies nothing in 300 s. So every pass in all ten files is a declared ROUTE, and what the
#      campaign measures is a geometry an author flew, never one a pilot chose.
#   2. THERE IS NO SCRAMBLE (gap C6). A `.fbm` spawns aircraft, it does not alert them, so the
#      sub-15-minute reaction that IS the real mission is in none of these files. Where a sortie needs
#      two flights at different readiness it expresses the delay as DISTANCE (sortie 10).
#   3. THERE IS NO RULES-OF-ENGAGEMENT STATE (gap C19). Nothing in the tree says "weapons hold".
#      `objective no_fire` JUDGES a hold, it cannot IMPOSE one, and a pilot on `route` never consults
#      its own warning receiver at all. The discipline this campaign is about is therefore OBSERVED and
#      never COMMANDED - sortie 07 is where that is measured rather than asserted.
#   4. `FBMissionRunner` ends the whole run at the first flight-monitor K.O. (`FirstFlightKo`).
#
# THE SECOND ARCHETYPE THE ANCHOR NAMES, beside the lone ELINT aircraft: an escorted bomber (Swedish
# fighters intercepting a Tu-22M escorted by Su-35S, [T4]). FlightBox has `tu95` as a mover row, and
# what makes it worth having is one number: its 50.1 m span [T4] against the escorts' 11.36 m. THE
# SUBSTITUTION IS NAMED: a Tu-95MS is not a Tu-22M and the escorts are MiG-29s rather than Su-35s,
# because those are the rows the tree has. THE TAIL TURRET IS NOT MODELLED (doc/modules/air/module.md
# A6), so a stern conversion here is against a bomber that cannot shoot back.
# THE ESCORTS DO NOT RADIATE (`set n019_emission off`, the documented power-up state) and they do not
# engage: this is a policing intercept and not a fight. What they do is HEAR - `set rwr on`, so the
# price the interceptors pay for their own radar is visible on the other side.
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: with three contacts and two interceptors, does the flight sort onto the
# SUBJECT or onto the ESCORT? The sort has no notion of "the one that matters" - there is no lead
# tasking (gap C15) and `FBFlightPicture` ranks by a time-to-arrive cost and nothing else.
#
# READING RULE (binding): exit 0 with all THREE `identify` objectives met is the pass. Read the
# `flight SORT_ASSIGN` lines and their `track` numbers - a radar track number is assigned in REGISTRY
# ORDER, i.e. in mission declaration order (`FBRadarSystem::ScanFrame`), so track 1 is the bomber,
# 2 and 3 the escorts - then `vis IDENTIFIED ... type=` per track, and the three `mission IDENTIFIED`
# lines with their ranges. A FAIL would mean an interceptor fired.
#
# MEASURED (fingerprint 050476a20acd4b31, exit 0): THE FLIGHT SORTS ONTO THE SUBJECT - AND THE SPLIT
# LASTS EXACTLY ONE SECOND.
#   t=1.9  `SORT_ASSIGN unit=q4lead track=1 free=1 dup=0`   the lead takes the bomber, one contact free
#   t=1.9  `SORT_ASSIGN unit=q4two  track=2 free=0 dup=0`   the wingman takes an escort - A REAL SPLIT
#   t=2.9  `SORT_ASSIGN unit=q4two  track=1 was=2 dup=1`    and gives it up for the bomber
#   The cost function is time-to-arrive and nothing else, so both aircraft converge on the nearest
#   contact and the duplicate is FLAGGED and not resolved. It sorts onto the subject, and it does so
#   for the wrong reason: the bomber is not the one that matters, it is the one that is closest.
#   THE EYE, THREE AIRCRAFT IN ONE FORMATION, AND THE 4.4 : 1 SPAN RATIO DECIDING EVERYTHING:
#     the Tu-95 (span 50.1 m):  `vis CONTACT` at t=1.9 on the FIRST LOOK at 8 869 m, `vis RECOGNISED`
#                               t=87.0, `vis IDENTIFIED` t=108.0 at 2 049 m - twice the An-26's range
#                               and 3.5 x the F-16's
#     the MiG-29s (span 11.36 m): `vis CONTACT` at t=112.0 and t=114.0 at ~3 km and NEVER RECOGNISED -
#                               the pass closes to 1 734 m and 1 600 m, and recognition needs ~730 m
#   A BOMBER IS NAMED AT 2 km WHILE THE FIGHTERS FLYING FORMATION ON IT ARE ANONYMOUS SHAPES AT 1.6 km.
#   Nothing about that was tuned; it is one law and two published spans.
#   The verdict: all three `identify` objectives met, `no_fire` met on both, exit 0. Closest approach
#   348.7 m to the bomber, 1 734.0 m and 1 599.9 m to the escorts.

unit q4lead
  team friendly
  module f16
  flight qra 1
  spawn 55.90450 19.05419 6200 90.0 500   # 9.1 km in trail of the bomber, 300 m below
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set datalink on
  set fcr_mode acm_hud                       # it looks, and the LOOK is not the verdict
  set fcr_range_nm 40
  set iff_interrogator on                    # the challenging half of the APX-113
  wp 55.90450 20.60000 6200 500
  objective identify unit q4sub range 2000 hold 20
  objective identify unit q4esc1 range 2000 hold 20
  objective identify unit q4esc2 range 2000 hold 20
  objective no_fire

unit q4two
  team friendly
  module f16
  flight qra 2
  spawn 55.88784 19.05419 6350 90.0 500
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set datalink on
  set fcr_mode acm_hud                       # it looks, and the LOOK is not the verdict
  set fcr_range_nm 40
  set iff_interrogator on                    # the challenging half of the APX-113
  set task formation
  objective no_fire

unit q4sub
  team neutral
  module tu95
  spawn 55.90000 19.20000 6500 90.0 383      # Tu-95MS cruise 710 km/h = 383 kt [T4]
  set iff_xpdr off
  wp 55.90000 20.45000 6500 383

unit q4esc1
  team neutral
  module mig29
  spawn 55.91950 19.20000 7000 90.0 383      # 1 667 m abeam the interceptor's track
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set n019_emission off                      # the documented power-up state: it does not paint
  set rwr on                                 # but it HEARS
  set iff_xpdr off
  wp 55.91950 20.45000 7000 383

unit q4esc2
  team neutral
  module mig29
  spawn 55.88950 19.20000 7000 90.0 383      # 1 667 m abeam the interceptor's track
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set n019_emission off                      # the documented power-up state: it does not paint
  set rwr on                                 # but it HEARS
  set iff_xpdr off
  wp 55.88950 20.45000 7000 383

unit q4rad
  module p18
  team friendly
  spawn 55.80000 21.05000 ground 0.0 0
  set emcon free

unit q4port
  module target_hard
  team friendly
  spawn 55.70000 21.13000 ground 0.0 0
